[?2004hroot@4b540f928f29:/app# cat >> README.md <<'MD'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Resume and recovery
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> `last_id` is the last **committed** document ID (not an offset or the next ID).
[?2004l[?2004h> The worker resumes strictly after it, preserving the source's gaps and deleted-row
[?2004l[?2004h> filtering. SIGTERM, SIGINT, and SIGHUP request a stop between documents; the worker
[?2004l[?2004h> commits the finished prefix and exits 75 when incomplete.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> A commit first flushes and fsyncs shard data and directory entries, then atomically
[?2004l[?2004h> replaces and fsyncs the checkpoint. The checkpoint includes a `position` containing
[?2004l[?2004h> the final shard number and byte offset. On restart, that shard is truncated to the
[?2004l[?2004h> committed offset and any later shards are removed before appending new work.
[?2004l[?2004h> Thus a hard kill can discard uncommitted work but cannot skip committed rows or
[?2004l[?2004h> replay rows into duplicates. Shard boundaries have no semantic meaning; a resumed
[?2004l[?2004h> run starts a new shard. A completed checkpoint is idempotent.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Old checkpoints without a durable position cannot be trusted (the old writer
[?2004l[?2004h> checkpointed buffered rows); they trigger a one-time rebuild. Malformed checkpoints
[?2004l[?2004h> or missing committed bytes fail closed rather than silently resetting progress.
[?2004l[?2004h> Use `./jobs clean` only when intentionally requesting a new full rebuild.
[?2004l[?2004h> MD
